  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 254 AM EDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\n- Showers ending, then dry for a few days\n\nRegional radar shows much of the rain as dissipated north of a\nKalamazoo to Mason line. South of there, local radar showed a\ndeformation band of light to moderate rain. This will persist this\nmorning before it moves southeast out of the cwa late this\nmorning as the upper trough axis swings through. High pressure \nwill then build into the cwa and we'll see sunny weather return\nMonday and Tuesday. \n\nHighs will top out around 80 late this afternoon and then we'll be\nin the lower to mid 80s Monday and Tuesday. Humidity will be held\nin check Monday and Tuesday as dewpoints will remain in the 50s. \n\n- Mid week precipitation chances and beyond\n\nThe upper ridge will move east during the middle of the week and\nan upper trough pattern will develop and persist through next\nweekend. That's generally an unsettled pattern for the Great Lakes\nas short waves rotate through the trough and act as a catalyst \nfor shower/storm development. Additionally, the upper trough \nhelps to push cold fronts through the region and they too help to \ngenerate storms. Also true, is this type of pattern is difficult \nto pin down timing wise as some of the short waves can be \ninnocuous and some more pronounced. So, as we look toward mid \nweek, models are showing a cold front drifting south across the \nLower Peninsula. We'll likely see some shower/storm development \nWednesday afternoon/evening and then again over the weekend as \nanother front moves south across the state. \n\nGiven upper troughing in place, highs will remain in the 80s.",
